  <h2 id="s-1">Who uses it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Users</strong> — everyone has the same kind of account. Report lost
        or found items, file claims on other people's found items, and track your
        own reports. The person who posts a report approves or rejects claims on it,
        and marks the item returned once it is handed over.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-2">How matching works</h2>
  <p>When you file a report, WhereITis quietly looks for reports of the opposite
  kind in the same category within a set window (default 30 days). Each candidate
  is scored on simple, explainable rules: same category, same location, close
  dates, overlapping words in the title and description. The score and every
  reason are shown to you on the report page. There is no AI, no black box — just
  a checklist you can read.</p>

  <h2 id="s-3">What this midterm does NOT include</h2>
  <p>This is a CCIT 106 midterm project, built inside Weeks 1–8. The following are
  deliberately out of scope and will be added in later weeks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Email or SMS notifications</li>
    <li>Two-factor authentication</li>
    <li>Native mobile app</li>
    <li>Live chat</li>
    <li>Payment or fine collection</li>
    <li>Integration with the Registrar's student information system</li>
    <li>AI image recognition to match items</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="s-4">Gender-fair by design</h2>
  <p>WhereITis does not collect gender, civil status, religion, birthday, or any
  government ID number. None of these is read by any screen or report in the
  system, so under the proportionality principle of RA 10173 they are not
  collected at all. The interface uses <em>chairperson</em>, <em>staff</em>,
  <em>first-year student</em>, and <em>they / their</em> — never <em>chairman</em>,
  <em>manpower</em>, <em>freshman</em>, or a generic <em>he</em>.</p>

  <h2 id="s-5">Credits</h2>
  <p>Built by CCIT 106 students at Camarines Sur Polytechnic Colleges, College of
  Computer Studies. Source code is MIT-licensed.</p>

